<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Hawaii</h1><p class="st-meta">1795 CE – 1893 CE · capital Honolulu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The island kingdom united by Kamehameha I, which became an internationally recognised constitutional monarchy before American businessmen overthrew it in 1893.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> was divided among rival chiefdoms, including the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chiefdom-of-hawaii-island">Chiefdom of Hawaii Island</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chiefdom-of-maui">Chiefdom of Maui</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chiefdom-of-oahu">Chiefdom of Oahu</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha</a>, a chief from Hawaii Island armed with Western guns and advisers, conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nuuanu">Battle of Nuʻuanu</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oahu</a> in 1795. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauai">Kauaʻi</a> accepted his overlordship peacefully in 1810, completing unification.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha</a>'s death the kapu system was abolished in 1819, and American Protestant missionaries arrived in 1820. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii">Kamehameha III</a> granted the first constitution in 1840 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mahele">Great Māhele</a> of 1848 created private land ownership, much of it soon held by foreigners. Sandalwood, whaling and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> drove the economy, and plantation labour brought Chinese, Japanese and Portuguese migrants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a> became the capital in 1845.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar</a> planters forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet-constitution">Bayonet Constitution</a> on King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">Kalākaua</a> in 1887. When Queen Liliʻuokalani sought a new constitution, a committee backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-marine-corps">US Marines</a> overthrew her in January 1893 and set up the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/provisional-government-of-hawaii">Provisional Government of Hawaii</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-hawaii.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Hawaii" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Dbenbenn · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hawaii.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1795 CE – 1893 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Honolulu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kamehameha I, Kamehameha III, Liliʻuokalani</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism, Hawaiian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hawaiian, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hawaiian Kingdom, Kingdom of Hawaiʻi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1844&amp;focus=kingdom-of-hawaii" aria-label="Kingdom of Hawaii on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="439 1390 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Hawaii lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M759 1646L790 1661M759 1646L801 1692M759 1646L759 1646"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M790 1661h0M801 1692h0M759 1646h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M759 1646h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M759 1646h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Honolulu. <a href="/atlas?year=1844&amp;focus=kingdom-of-hawaii">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
